'''Atla Castejón''' ({{langx|mao|Alt-Kaséxo}}), alternatively known as '''Western Castejón''' ({{Lang|bl|Castejón Occidental}}) or '''Upper Castejón''' ({{Lang|bl|Castejón Superior}}) is a geographic and cultural region within the [[Castejón Metropolis]], referring to the upland, non-built-up portions of the metropolis that lie beyond the dense coastal core. ''Alta Castejón'' is not a separate administrative division — its territory falls within the boundaries of the 12 [[Boroughs of Castejón|boroughs]]-[[Cantons of Balisca|cantons]] of Castejón. With an estimated population of approximately 8,684,194 as of May 2026, Atla Castejón accounts for roughly 25.8% of the Castejón Metropolis' total population of 33,620,715.

Atla Castejón occupies the foothills and upland valleys of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], the low mountain chain that rises behind the [[Alpujarra]], the coastal plain on which the vast majority of the Metropolitan population resides. Where the cantons are relatively dense, flat, and coastal — the urban core of the [[Silicon Coast]] — Atla Castejón is suburban, hilly, and inland: a landscape of [[W:Commuter town|commuter towns]], [[W:Satellite city|satellite cities]], agricultural settlements, and [[W:Forest reserve|forest reserves]] that stretches from the urban fringe of [[El Capitán]], [[Llanito]], and [[Mocambu]] westward into the mountains.

The distinction between the built-up core and ''Alta Castejón'' is the fundamental social and cultural divide within the Castejón Metropolis. In everyday speech, residents of the cantons refer to their area simply as "Castejón" or "la vila" (the city), while Atla Castejón is ''la Superior'' (the Upper) or, colloquially, ''la serra'' (the hills). Residents of Atla Castejón, in turn, refer to the cantons as ''la costa'' (the coast) or ''abaixo'' (below, down). The spatial metaphor — up vs. down, mountain vs. coast — encodes a social hierarchy: the cantons are the center, the economic engine, the "real" Castejón; Atla Castejón is the periphery, or the "bedroom".

== Administrative structure ==

The Castejón Metropolis is governed by the [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón]] ({{Langx|bl|Consejo Metropolitana de Castejón}}), which governs the entire metropolitan area of {{Convert|39839|km2|sqmi}} through the 12 cantons. Each canton spans both a dense built-up area on the coastal plain and a large non-built-up territory in the mountains and reserves. The cantonal governments devolve authority within their non-built-up territories to municipalities and communes, which handle local governance while the canton retains overall jurisdiction.

The administrative structure within a typical canton is hierarchical:

* '''Cantons''' - the cantonal council governs the entire cantonal territory, including both the built-up core and the non-built-up periphery. The council sets cantonal policy on transit, housing, environmental regulation, and economic development. In the built-up areas, the cantonal government exercises direct authority; in the non-built-up areas, it devolves most day-to-day governance to the communes.

* '''Communes''' — The non-built-up portions of each canton contain municipalities (towns and small cities in the valleys) and communes (smaller settlements and cooperative agricultural communities). These have their own councils and mayors, responsible for local services — roads, schools, water systems, land-use planning — within their boundaries. They are part of the canton for purposes of regional planning, transit coordination, and revenue sharing, but they retain a degree of local autonomy that the built-up areas have largely ceded to the cantonal government.

* '''Reserve districts''' — The uninhabited mountain and watershed areas within each canton are administered as reserve districts by the Metropolitan Council in coordination with the cantonal government. These areas — which include the [[Serra Nevada Ecological Reserve]], the upland watersheds, and other protected territories — have no permanent population and are managed for watershed protection, hydroelectric generation, and recreational access. The reserve districts are the subject of persistent political tension: Atla Castejón residents argue that the reserves' restrictions on development impose costs on their municipalities that are borne locally but benefit the coastal core.

== Geography ==
{{Main|Geography of Castejón}}

[[File:Kasolé new town.jpg|thumb|300x300px|The [[Planned communities in Balisca|planned community]] of [[Kasolé]], located in central [[Mocambu]] near the [[Ijasi river]].]]
Alta Castejón occupies the western and upland portions of the Castejón Metropolis, rising from the coastal plain at elevations of 0–10 meters to the foothills and ridges of the [[Serra Nevada]] at elevations of 200–800 meters. The transition from built-up core to Alta is gradual — the eastern edges of the [[Llanito]], [[El Capitán]], and [[Mocambu]] boroughs blur into the first ''Altano'' municipalities with no clear boundary — but the landscape changes rapidly: the relatively flat grid of the plains gives way to winding roads, forested hillsides, and valleys.

The region is defined by its watersheds. Three major river valleys — the [[Ijasi river]], the [[Alagón River|Alagón river]], and the [[Riu d'Or]] — cut through the Serra Nevada from west to east, draining the upland forests and carrying water to the coastal plain. The valleys are the population corridors of Atla Castejón: the major municipalities are strung along the valley floors, connected by the [[Castejón Metro]], commuter rail lines, and the most populous by the [[AVB|AVB high-speed rail system]], which all run roughly parallel with the rivers down to the built-up core. 

The ridges between the valleys are largely forested — part of the [[Serra Nevada Ecological Reserve]], the largest protected area within the metropolis — and sparsely populated.

The built-up area of the metropolis — {{Convert|6,038.4|km2|sqmi|abbr=on}} out of the total {{Convert|39839|km2|sqmi|abbr=on}}— concentrates the vast majority of the population on the coastal plain. The remaining 33,800 km², while part of the same boroughs, is overwhelmingly closer to their nature states: mountain, protected forests, watersheds, and the narrow river valleys where Atla Castejón's municipalities are located.

== Economy ==
{{Main|Economy of Balisca}}

Alta Castejón's economy is defined by its relationship with the metropolitan core; the majority of its working-age population commutes to the built-up areas for employment — a daily migration that shapes the region's rhythm, its transit infrastructure, and its identity. The [[Castejón Metro]] [[W:Rapid transit|rapid transit]] system, [[ComMuni]] ([[W:Bus rapid transit|bus rapid transit]], [[W:Light rail|light rail]], and [[W:Water transport|water transport]]) and the commuter rail network (sometimes including the [[AVB|AVB high-speed rail]]), make up the circulatory system of the metropolis.

However, Atla Castejón is not merely a [[W:Bedroom community|bedroom community]]. The region has its own economic base:

=== Agriculture ===
[[File:AVB Transmatarrañya.jpg|thumb|300x300px|Terraced polycultural fields in the [[Alagón River|Alagón river]] valley. The rich [[W:Alluvial soil|alluvial soils]] of the [[Alpujarra]], fed by upland sediment and irrigation from the watershed, result in one of the most productive agricultural areas in [[Balisca]].]]
The valleys of the [[Serra Nevada]] enjoy a climate of warm, dry summers and mild, wet winters, with exceptionally rich [[W:Alluvial soil|alluvial soils]] deposited over millennia by the three rivers. The result is one of the most productive agricultural regions in Balisca, with the valley floors and lower slopes support intensive cooperative agriculture. This productivity is not achieved through conventional [[W:Monoculture|monoculture]]. The Atla's agricultural system is rooted in [[W:Permaculture|permaculture]] and indigenous [[W:Regenerative agriculture|regenerative practices]], maintained by these communities as both an ecological philosophy and a cultural inheritance. 

The valley floors are not given over to single-crop fields but to [[W:Polyculture|polycultural]] food forests — layered systems where rice paddies share contour swales with nitrogen-fixing legumes, where olive and almond groves are interplanted with cover crops and pollinator corridors, and where the mycorrhizal networks that bind the soil are treated as cooperative infrastructure as essential as the irrigation canals. The [[Alpujarran Agricultural Cooperative]], one of the largest [[W:Agricultural cooperative|agricultural cooperative]] [[W:Cooperative federations|federations]] in the country, coordinates production across communes in multiple cantons and operates the [[Mercado de l'Alta|''Mercado de l'Atla'']], the primary wholesale market for upland produce.

The south-facing slopes above the valley floors are planted in olives, producing an oil regarded as the finest in the [[Levante, Balisca|Levante]]. The olive production is a Maroon tradition: the first trees were planted by communities who brought cuttings from the Bayaran plantations, grafting the Mesogean tree onto Kaftian agricultural knowledge. The oldest groves are still known by the names of the refugees that planted them three centuries ago. Almonds thrive on the drier upper slopes, and the almond harvest in late summer is one of the great communal events of the ''Atla'' calendar — entire cooperatives turn out to shake the trees, and the [[Alpujarran railway]] runs dedicated trains for the harvest trains to carry the crop to the coast.

Beyond the three staples, the Atla produces small quantities of [[W:Saffron|saffron]] in the Riu d'Or valley — the hand-harvested crocus commands a premium that makes it the wealthiest agricultural area in the Atla — alongside wine grapes on the terraced hillsides and cut flowers under hydroelectric-powered climate control. In the more isolated Maroon-founded settlements, agricultural cooperatives maintain heritage plantings of [[W:Sweet potato|sweet potato]], [[W:Cassava|cassava]], and indigenous bean varieties alongside the commercial crops, a dual-cropping system that preserves [[W:Food sovereignty|food sovereignty]] while producing for the market. These heritage crops are consumed within the cooperative and distributed through the ''komida'' system, not sold on the metropolitan market.

The [[Atla Castejón Agricultural Cooperative]] coordinates production across all three valleys and operates the [[Mercat de l'Atla]] (Market of the Upper), the primary wholesale market for upland produce. Every morning, cooperative trucks carry rice, oil, almonds, saffron, wine, and flowers down to the coastal distribution network; every evening, they return with the manufactured goods and consumer products the Atla does not produce for itself.

=== Tech, tourism, and hydroelectric power ===
Several major tech companies have established satellite campuses in Atla Castejón, drawn by lower land costs, proximity to the forest reserves, and the availability of [[W:Hydroelectric power|hydroelectric power]]. The [[Concorda Tecnológica de l'Atla]] (Technological Concord of the Upper), a federation of Atlano tech cooperatives, has lobbied for improved transit and [[W:Digital infrastructure|digital infrastructure]]. The tech presence is a source of ambivalence: the campuses bring employment and investment, but they also bring a more fast-paced, coastal culture that sits uneasily alongside the more traditional communal tradition.

The [[Serra Nevada Ecological Reserve]], the upland rivers, and the cooler mountain climate make Alta Castejón the primary recreational destination for core residents. Weekend and holiday traffic from the coast is a significant economic driver, supporting hotels, restaurants, and outdoor recreation companies. The upland watersheds of Alta Castejón provide approximately 35% of the metropolis's electricity through a network of small-scale and large-scale hydroelectric installations managed by the [[Cooperativa d'Enerxía de l'Alt]] (Energy Cooperative of the Upper).  

The hydroelectric infrastructure, built primarily in the 1950s and 1960s, is a source of both pride and political tension: ''Altanos'' argue that the metropolis depends on their water and power, particuarly in the case of [[Aipi]] and [[tba]], but does not adequately compensate them for the environmental costs of [[W:Watershed management|watershed management]].

== Social dynamics ==
{{Main|Social inequality in Castejón}}

[[File:Alt_Castejón_Maroon_community.jpg|thumb|300x300px|A Maroon-descended cooperative settlement in the [[Mocambu]]. The communal housing pattern — dwellings arranged around a shared courtyard and ''komida'' — reflects the continuity of Maroon social organization in the upland municipalities.]]The relationship between the coastal core and Atla Castejón is the defining social tension within the [[Castejón Metropolis]]. The core is wealthier, denser, more cosmopolitan, and more multilingual — the center of the Silicon Coast's global economy. Atla Castejón is more residential, more homogeneous, more [[Maroniko]]-speaking, and more rooted in the cultural traditions of the [[Maroons|Maroon diaspora]] — the place where core workers sleep, but not where they work or play.

The upland valleys of the Serra Nevada were first settled in the 17th and 18th centuries by communities of self-liberated Kaftians and their descendants — people who had escaped the Bayaran plantations and established themselves in the interior, beyond the reach of colonial authority. The communities of the Serra Nevada maintained their autonomy for generations, developing the cooperative agricultural practices, the communal housing patterns, and the syncretic spiritual traditions that still define the Atla's identity. When the metropolis expanded westward to include the valleys that make up ''Alta'' in the middle of the 19th century-following the failed [[Siege of Castejón]] during the [[Baliscan Civil War]], it absorbed these communities without displacing them — the result is a landscape where the commuter rail stations sit alongside Maroon-founded [[W:Quilombos|''quilombo'']].

=== Identity and terminology ===

=== Bioregionalism ===
{{Main|Bioregionalism}}
[[File:Aipi lower reservoir.jpg|left|thumb|Lower reservoir of [[Aipi]], Balisca's largest [[W:Reservoir|freshwater reservoir]]]]The most consequential dispute between the Atla and the core concerns the management of the [[Serra Nevada Forest Reserve]], which occupies much of Atla Castejón's land area. The reserve is administered by the Metropolitan Authority under the confederal bioregional framework, which demarcates Balisca's first-level administrative divisions on the basis of bioregionalism — [[W:Watershed|watersheds]], ecosystems, and [[W:Ecoregion|natural boundaries]] rather than arbitrary political lines. In principle, bioregionalism should favor the Atla: the framework recognizes that the [[Serra Nevada]] watershed is a single ecological unit, and that the communities who inhabit it have the deepest knowledge of and stake in its management. In practice, the Metropolitan Authority invokes the same bioregional principle to justify top-down administration of the reserve for the benefit of the entire metropolis — including the downstream coastal core, which depends on the watershed for its water supply, hydroelectric generation, and flood control.

The dispute is not between bioregionalism and its opposite: both sides claim the bioregional principle but disagree on its application. The Atla position — articulated most forcefully by the [[Atla Watershed Council]], a coalition of communal assemblies, cooperatives, and community organizations — holds that bioregional governance means governance by the people who live within the bioregion. The Serra Nevada's upland communities have managed these forests for centuries, since the first Indigenous settlements established their own traditions of watershed stewardship, fire management, and forest rotation. The bioregional principle, they argue, requires that this [[W:Traditional knowledge|traditional local knowledge]] and local [[W:Self-determination|self-determination]] be respected rather than overridden by a metropolitan authority that treats the watershed as a resource to be managed for downstream extraction. The restrictions on development, construction, and resource extraction that the Metropolitan Council imposes on the reserve impose measurable costs on Atla municipalities — costs that are borne locally while the coast enjoys the watershed protection and recreational access without equivalent land-use constraints. The environmental justice argument is inseparable from the historical one: the Indigenous communities who first protected these forests did not surrender their right to use them when the metropolis designated them as reserves.

The Metropolitan Council's position — shared by the coastal cantonal councils and the [[Concorda Tecnológica de l'Atla]], the federation of tech cooperatives — holds that bioregionalism means managing the bioregion as a whole, not merely the part where one happens to live. The Serra Nevada watershed is a single system: conditions in the upland forests affect the [[W:Water quality|water quality]], flood risk, and hydroelectric output on which the entire metropolis depends. The Atla communities are stakeholders, but they are not the only stakeholders; the coast is downstream, and downstream effects are consequential. Bioregional management, the Council argues, requires coordination across the entire watershed, which is why the Authority, rather than individual cantons and communes, administers the reserve. To devolve reserve management to the Atla municipalities would, in this view, privilege local self-determination over systemic integrity.

The dispute is a disagreement within bioregionalism itself: between bioregionalism as local self-determination (the Atla position) and bioregionalism as holistic systems management (the Metropolitan Authority position). Both positions draw on the bioregional principle, and neither has been reconciled with the other, because the Serra Nevada functions simultaneously as a residential area and as a watershed sustaining life beyond its boundaries.

==== Controlled burning ====
The same tension finds its most visceral expression in the dispute over controlled burning. Indigenous communities of the Alpujarra have practiced cultural burning for over three centuries — low-intensity, deliberately set fires that clear undergrowth, recycle nutrients, reduce [[W:Fuel load|fuel loads]], and promote the regeneration of fire-adapted species. The practice is integral for [[W:Indigenous land management|Indigenous land management]]: the burns are scheduled according to the Kuarupí ecological calendar, conducted by trained fire cooperatives, and followed by systematic monitoring of regrowth. Without them, the Serra Nevada's forests accumulate dangerous levels of dead wood and ladder fuels, increasing the risk of catastrophic [[W:Wildfire|wildfire]] — a risk that the Atla communities, who live in the forest, take seriously. The controlled burn is consider an act of care: "burn the forest so the forest does not burn you."

The smoke generated by controlled burns affects the coastal core. The Serra Nevada sits upwind of the coastal plain, with prevailing northeasterly winds carrying smoke into the Castejón Metropolis during burn seasons — typically late autumn and early spring, when conditions are cool and damp enough to control the fire. During burn periods, the core's air quality deteriorates: particulate counts increase, hospital admissions for respiratory conditions rise, and outdoor commercial activity is disrupted. The Metropolitan Council issues air quality alerts during burn periods, and tech companies often report increased [[W:Air filtration|air filtration]] costs at [[W:Data center|data centers]].
[[File:1989 Trinacria firestorm.jpeg|thumb|The [[1989 Trinacria firestorm]], which caused significant damage in [[Trinacria]] and resulted in the deaths of 183 people]]
The core's position is that burns must be restricted, scheduled around favorable wind conditions, or replaced with mechanical fuel reduction. The Atla's position is that mechanical thinning cannot replicate the ecological effects of fire — including nutrient cycling, seed germination, and pest and disease control — and that restricting burns to wind conditions that do not affect the core would, in practice, prevent burning altogether, as the prevailing wind almost always blows from the mountains toward the coast. The Atla communities cite the [[1989 Trinacria firestorm|1989 Trinacria firestome]] as evidence: the fire, which burned for three weeks, produced smoke that blanketed the metropolis and consumed approximately twelve times the acreage that a full season of controlled burns would have treated.

The controlled burning dispute parallels the watershed management dispute: both concern the relationship between the mountains and the coast, the distribution of costs and benefits, and whether the bioregion is governed by its residents or managed for the broader system it sustains. The burn dispute is, however, more immediately tangible than the watershed dispute, as smoke affects air quality directly and cannot be contained within administrative boundaries.

